Writing, coding, blogging, office work, documentation, reporting all needs one to type on a keyboard. This causes health issues like carpel tunnel syndrome, pain in hands and fingers etc. I know this pain very intimately.

Here is the Python code to create your own dictation program which runs offline. Just speak into your headphone’s mic and it converts your words to text and saves it in a text file.

You will need to install the Python libraries — vosk, pyaudio.

Vosk is a speech recognition toolkit that provides streaming API for accurate speech recognition and speaker identification. It supports 20+ languages and dialects — English, Indian English, German, French, Spanish, Portuguese, Chinese, Russian, Turkish, Vietnamese, Italian, Dutch, Catalan, Arabic, Greek, Farsi, Filipino, Ukrainian, Kazakh, Swedish, Japanese, Esperanto, Hindi, Czech, Polish, Uzbek, Korean, Breton, Gujarati. More to come. It works offline. You can use it on lightweight devices — Raspberry Pi, and mobile phones both Android and iOS. It offers multiple language models ranging from size of 40MB to 16BG. Most small model allow dynamic vocabulary reconfiguration. Big models are static the vocabulary can not be modified in runtime. Check out all the models that can be used with vosk here.

If you want to use the application offline, download the approriate model’s zip file to your computer. Unzip the file and this unzipped folder is used by the program to create the model and generate text. Else if you wish to work online, you can just mention the language in the vock.Model() and the appropriate model is downloaded at runtime. (Code below demonstrates this further.)

You can find many examples to use vosk here.

PyAudio provides Python bindings for PortAudio v19, the cross-platform audio I/O library. With PyAudio, you can easily use Python to play and record audio on a variety of platforms, such as GNU/Linux, Microsoft Windows, and Apple macOS. PyAudio is distributed under the MIT License.

